WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.310
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:11.200
This paper is from 2007, from ten years ago
and I am sure you'll be surprised by how well

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.810
it is still holding up to today's standards.

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:19.270
For me, it was one of the works that foreshadowed
the incredible power of data-driven learning

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:20.270
algorithms.

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:26.230
So, let's grab an image, and cut a sizeable
part out of it, and try to algorithmically

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:28.910
fill it with data that makes sense.

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:33.770
Removing a drunk photobombing friend from
your wedding picture, or a building blocking

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:40.170
a beautiful view to the sea are excellent,
and honestly, painfully real examples of this.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:44.180
This problem we like to call image completion
or image inpainting.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:50.220
But mathematically, this may sound like crazy
talk, who really knows what information should

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.530
be there in these holes, let alone a computer?

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:58.270
The first question is, why would we have to
synthesize all these missing details from

00:00:58.270 --> 00:00:59.270
scratch?

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:04.400
Why not start looking around in an enormous
database of photographs and look for something

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:05.400
similar?

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:10.140
For instance, let's unleash a learning algorithm
on one million images.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:15.220
And if we do so, we could find that there
may be photographs in the database that are

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:16.840
from the same place.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.830
But then, what about the illumination?

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.409
The lighting may be different!

00:01:21.409 --> 00:01:26.700
Well, this is an enormous database, so then,
we pick a photo that was taken at a similar

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.830
time of the day and use that information!

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:34.180
And as we can see in the results, the technique
works like magic!

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:35.180
Awesome!

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:39.800
It doesn't require user made annotations or
any sort of manual labor.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:43.479
These results were way, way ahead of the competition.

00:01:43.479 --> 00:01:48.170
And sometimes, the algorithm proposes a set
of solutions that we can choose from.

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:54.290
The main challenge of this solution is finding
similar images within the database, and fortunately,

00:01:54.290 --> 00:02:01.640
even a trivial technique that we call nearest
neighbor search can rapidly eliminate 99.99%

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.610
of the dissimilar images.

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:08.959
The paper also discusses some of the failure
cases, which arise mostly from the lack of

00:02:08.959 --> 00:02:14.310
high-level semantic information, for instance,
when we have to finish people, which is clearly

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:19.750
not what this technique is meant to do, unless
it's a statue of a famous person with many

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:22.290
photographs taken in the database.

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:28.030
Good that we're in 2017, and we know that
plenty of research groups are already working

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:33.279
on this, and I wouldn't be surprised to see
a generative adversarial network-based technique

00:02:33.279 --> 00:02:35.730
to pop up for this in the very near future.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:39.730
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

